# Response headers for the RBI weekly dashboard.
#
# This is the port of the [[headers]] blocks that used to live in netlify.toml,
# moved to where Cloudflare reads them: `_headers` in the assets directory. The
# file itself is never served (Cloudflare parses it and answers 404 for the path
# — verified on the Dashboard Portal), so the policy is public and the file is
# not.
#
# Netlify's per-function `timeout = 20/26` settings have no equivalent here and
# need none: a Worker invocation is not cut off at ten seconds the way a
# synchronous Netlify function was, so the scrapers that walk several upstream
# weeks or pages sequentially have more room, not less.
#
# The site is fully static with same-origin scripts/styles/fonts only; the
# function API lives under /data/* and /.netlify/functions/*.

# frame-ancestors names the Dashboard Portal alongside 'self': the portal shows
# this dashboard in a preview frame, and 'none' made every preview refuse to
# load. The legacy X-Frame-Options header is gone for the same reason - a DENY
# there contradicts the allowance below. The allowance stays origin-scoped to
# the portal rather than `*`, so no other site can frame us.
#
# The portal's workers.dev hostname used to be listed here beside its Pages
# address, because both hosts served it and a frame's origin is whichever it
# loaded from. The Workers are gone; the Pages address is the only one left.
/*

  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self' https://dashboards-home-portal.pages.dev; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# Long-cache the vendored libraries and the icons (they change only with a
# deploy).
#
# Deliberately NOT `immutable`: these filenames carry no hash or version segment
# (chart.umd.js, gsap.min.js, three.min.js), and `immutable` promises the bytes
# at a URL can never change. The day one of them is bumped, every returning
# visitor would keep the old copy for a year with no way to revoke it — the
# promise the header makes is the thing that makes it unrecoverable. A week plus
# stale-while-revalidate still keeps repeat visits off the network.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=86400

/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=86400

# The self-hosted fonts. Same week-plus-stale policy, same reason.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=86400
